            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 1)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "5627dc6e-c943-439e-ab4a-4531b7f24264"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Alan Kay</strong><br>\nAlan Curtis Kay was born on May 17, 1940, in Springfield, Massachusetts. He showed an early interest in biology and mathematics and was an avid reader in his youth. His fascination with computing began when he stumbled upon the work of pioneers like Vannevar Bush and Norbert Wiener. Despite his early interests, it wasn't until college that he began to engage deeply with computers and programming.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Education and Inspiration</strong><br>\nKay pursued higher education at the University of Colorado, where he earned a Bachelor's degree in mathematics and molecular biology. He later obtained a Master's degree from the University of Utah in computer science. During his time at Utah, he was influenced by the work of Ivan Sutherland, who created Sketchpad, a revolutionary computer graphics program that inspired Kay's own ideas on object-oriented programming.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Invention of Object-Oriented Programming</strong><br>\nThe concept of object-oriented programming (OOP) was among Alan Kay's significant contributions to computer science. He developed OOP as a way to manage the complexity of software systems by encapsulating data and operations into objects. This concept became a fundamental paradigm in software development, influencing the creation of languages like Smalltalk, which he helped to develop at Xerox PARC.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
