      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Contribution to Personal Computing</strong><br>\nAt Xerox PARC (Palo Alto Research Center), Kay's vision for personal computing took shape. He was one of the key members of the team that developed the Alto, considered to be the first personal computer. Kay believed that computers should be user-friendly and accessible to everyone, including children, which led to his involvement in creating educational software.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Dynabook Concept</strong><br>\nKay is also known for conceptualizing the Dynabook, a portable and interactive personal computer that was designed for children's education. Although it was never built, the Dynabook's concept laid the groundwork for future portable computers, including laptops and tablets. His ideas were far ahead of their time, envisioning a world where computers would be an integral part of everyday life.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Alan Kay's Influence on Graphical User Interfaces</strong><br>\nAlan Kay played a significant role in the development of graphical user interfaces (GUIs), which allow users to interact with electronic devices using visual icons and a mouse rather than text-based commands. His work at Xerox PARC contributed to the evolution of interfaces that have become standard in modern computing.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
