          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Awards and Honors</strong><br>\nOver the years, Alan Kay has received numerous accolades for his contributions to computer science. He was awarded the Turing Award, often referred to as the \"Nobel Prize of Computing,\" in 2003. His pioneering work has earned him a place as a fellow of the American Academy of Arts and Sciences and the National Academy of Engineering.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Kay's Educational Philosophy</strong><br>\nAlan Kay is a proponent of using computers as an educational tool. He believes that through simulation and modeling, computers can offer an interactive learning experience. Kay advocates for what he calls \"powerful ideas,\" which are concepts that allow people to form a better understanding of the world through the use of computational thinking.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Continuing Work</strong><br>\nDespite his retirement from full-time academic positions, Kay continues to influence the field of computing through his ongoing research, writing, and speaking engagements. His current work focuses on improving the way people understand and utilize computers, reaffirming his commitment to making technology serve human needs more effectively.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
